ASSISTANT <thinking-content-7bbeb8e1441453ad999a0bbba8a46d4b>
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Explain nixos-generators / qemu derivation approach.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT </thinking-content-7bbeb8e1441453ad999a0bbba8a46d4b>
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT There are a few common approaches, depending on how much control you want:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ## 1. `nixos-generators` (easiest)
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Add `nixos-generators` to your flake inputs, then:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT nixosConfigurations.vm = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
ASSISTANT   system = "x86_64-linux";
ASSISTANT   modules = [
ASSISTANT     "${nixpkgs}/nixos/modules/virtualisation/qemu-vm.nix" # gives you a ready-to-boot VM
ASSISTANT     ./configuration.nix
ASSISTANT   ];
ASSISTANT };
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Then `nix build .#nixosConfigurations.vm.config.system.build.vm` produces a script that launches QEMU with the right kernel/initrd/cmdline.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ## 2. A custom derivation wrapping QEMU
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT If you want a derivation that *runs* QEMU (e.g. as a tool in your repo, which fits your `llm-agent` sandbox task):
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT pkgs.runCommand "qemu-vm-runner" { nativeBuildInputs = [ pkgs.makeWrapper ]; }
ASSISTANT   ''
ASSISTANT     mkdir -p $out/bin
ASSISTANT     makeWrapper ${pkgs.qemu}/bin/qemu-system-x86_64 $out/bin/run-vm \
ASSISTANT       --add-flags "-m 4096 -smp 2" \
ASSISTANT       --add-flags "-drive file=$out/disk.qcow2,if=virtio" \
ASSISTANT       --add-flags "-netdev user,id=n0,hostfwd=tcp::2222-:22 -device virtio-net-pci,netdev=n0"
ASSISTANT   ''
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ## 3. `pkgs.vmTools` / `makeTest` (for testing)
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT `nixpkgs#nixosTests` / `makeTestPyhton` from `nixos/lib/test-instrumentation.nix` spawn QEMU VMs with a NixOS guest — good for automated runs, but heavyweight.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ## Recommendation for your task
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Since your task is spawning a VM that runs a LangChain deep agent, the cleanest path is:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT 1. Define a NixOS configuration with a systemd service running the agent (using your existing `llm-agent/config.nix` / `setup.nix`).
ASSISTANT 2. Build it with `config.system.build.vm` (the `qemu-vm.nix` module) or `nixos-generators` to get a `qcow2` + launcher.
ASSISTANT 3. Wrap the launcher in a small derivation that exposes it as a "tool" (e.g. a script the agent/dev can invoke), forwarding the LLM provider config (API keys, endpoint) via environment variables or a shared directory.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT If you share `llm-agent/config.nix` and `llm-agent/setup.nix`, I can sketch how to wire them into the VM's NixOS configuration.
