ASSISTANT </thinking-content-7bbeb8e1441453ad999a0bbba8a46d4b>
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT No — `qemu-vm.nix` does **not** override or wipe `fileSystems`. It only *adds* entries (like `/` via the `virtualisation.fileSystems` option). Your entries would be merged in, not discarded.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT And your experiment proves something stronger: you set
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT fileSystems."/run/agent-keysxx" = 5;
ASSISTANT fileSystems."/run/agent-keys".fakeOption = "no";
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT If that module were being **evaluated at all**, NixOS would throw immediately — `fileSystems` is `attrsOf (submodule ...)`, so an integer value and an unknown option are hard type errors. The fact that evaluation succeeds silently means **the module containing these settings is not part of the system being evaluated/built**. The VM you're running was never generated from this code.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT So the question becomes: why isn't this module in the evaluation? Things to check, in order of likelihood:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **1. Is `llm-agent/setup.nix` actually imported/instantiated by the flake module?**
ASSISTANT Check `llm-agent/config.nix` and `flakeModule.nix`/`presets/flakeModule.nix` — is the setup module wired in only when an agent is *declared* in the flake config? If no `llm-agent` entry exists in the config (or the name doesn't match), the module may never be instantiated, and the app you're running comes from somewhere else entirely.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **2. Are you running the app this file produces?**
ASSISTANT Verify what `nix run` actually executes:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT nix eval .#apps.<system>.devtools/<agent-name>.program
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT and compare its store path freshness with a fresh build. If you're invoking an old `result` symlink or a script on your PATH, you'll boot a stale closure no matter how often you re-evaluate.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **3. Force-evaluate the VM config directly** to see whether your entries exist:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT nix eval <the-vm-attr>.config.fileSystems."/run/agent-keys" --json
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT (or add a temporary `builtins.trace`/`assert` in `setup.nix`, e.g. `assert builtins.trace (attrNames config.fileSystems) true;` style debugging in the module). If evaluation of that attribute fails with the type errors above — good, the module *is* live and you just have a build/run mismatch. If it evaluates cleanly without your entries, the module is dead code.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT My bet is on (1): the `llm-agent` flake module isn't instantiating `setup.nix` for your current configuration, so the `run-nixos-vm` you're launching was built from a different (older or default) definition. Could you add `llm-agent/config.nix` and the relevant part of `flakeModule.nix` to the chat so I can check the wiring?
