USER           virtualisation.memorySize = 2048;
USER           virtualisation.cores = 2;
USER 
USER           # Run the VM without a graphical display; the guest's
USER           # serial port (ttyS0) is connected directly to the
USER           # terminal that launched the VM, and the kernel console
USER           # is placed on that serial line.
USER           #virtualisation.graphics = false;
USER           #boot.kernelParams = [ "console=ttyS0" ];
USER 
USER           # Share the host's private configuration directory with
USER           # the guest through 9p. The mount is read-only; the agent
USER           # only needs to read its authentication key.
USER           virtualisation.qemu.options = [
USER             ''-virtfs local,path="$DEVTOOLS_CONFIG",mount_tag=devtools-config,security_model=none,readonly=on''
USER           ];
USER           boot.initrd.availableKernelModules = [ "9p" "9pnet_virtio" ];
USER           boot.kernelModules = [ "9p" "9pnet_virtio" ];
USER           fileSystems."/run/agent-keysxx" = 5;
USER           fileSystems."/run/agent-keys" = {
USER             #device = "devtools-config";
USER             fsType = "ext4";
USER             fakeOption = "no";
USER             #fsType = "9p";
USER             #options = [ "trans=virtio" "version=9p2000.L" "ro" "nofail" ];
USER           };
USER           users.users.nixos.hashedPassword = "$y$j9T$Ij.zgsiQ9UbG805vuIjz0/$qiZCBxun/3VJrAic6AGusQTlX4VN2W.Mp4LrFPrFZ6B";
USER           users.users.nixos.group = "nixos";
USER           users.users.nixos.extraGroups = [ "wheel" ];
USER           users.groups.nixos = { };
USER           users.users.nixos.isNormalUser = true;
USER 
USER           services.journald.extraConfig = ''
USER             ForwardToConsole=yes
USER             MaxLevelConsole=info
USER           '';
USER           # The agent service. Non-secret parameters are baked in;
USER           # the api key is read from the shared directory at runtime.
USER           # The service's output is forwarded to the kernel log so
USER           # that it shows up on the serial console, i.e. in the
USER           # terminal that launched the VM.
USER           systemd.services.llm-agent = {
USER             description = "LangChain deep agent";
USER             wantedBy = [ "multi-user.target" ];
USER             after = [ "network-online.target" ];
USER             wants = [ "network-online.target" ];
USER             environment = {
USER               OPENAI_BASE_URL = provider-endpoint-url;
USER               OPENAI_MODEL = provider-model;
USER               AGENT_USER = provider-user;
USER               AGENT_KEYS_DIR = "/run/agent-keys";
USER             };
USER             serviceConfig = {
USER               Type = "oneshot";
USER               RemainAfterExit = true;
USER               #StandardOutput = "kmsg";
USER               #StandardError = "kmsg";
USER             };
USER             script = ''
USER               exec ${agent-python}/bin/python ${agent-script}
USER             '';
USER             #unitConfig.OnFailure = [ "poweroff.target" ];
USER           };
USER         };
USER       }
USER     ];
USER   };
USER 
USER   /**
USER   The host-side launch script. It sources the tool specific
USER   environment (defining DEVTOOLS_CONFIG), warns if no llm
USER   provider is reachable on the host, and boots the VM with
USER   the private configuration directory shared through 9p.
USER   */
USER   launch-llm-agent = pkgs.writeScript "launch-${llm-agent-name}" ''
USER     #!${pkgs.bash}/bin/bash
USER     set -e
USER 
USER     source "${devtools-prelude.get-config llm-agent-name}"
USER 
USER     if ! ${pkgs.curl}/bin/curl -s "${provider-endpoint-url}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
USER       echo -e "\033[33mWarning: no llm provider seems to be reachable at '${provider-endpoint-url}'.\033[0m" >&2
USER       echo "If a lite-llm instance provides this model, launch it on the host first." >&2
USER     fi
USER 
USER     user_key=$(${key-script})
USER     user_key_dir="$DEVTOOLS_CONFIG/users/"
USER     mkdir -p "$user_key_dir"
USER     echo "$user_key" > "$user_key_dir/${provider-user}.key"
USER     export DEVTOOLS_CONFIG
USER     echo "Running: ${vm.config.system.build.vm}/bin/run-nixos-vm"
USER     exec ${vm.config.system.build.vm}/bin/run-nixos-vm
USER   '';
USER 
USER in
USER {
USER   config.apps."devtools/${llm-agent-name}" = {
USER     type = "app";
USER     program = "${launch-llm-agent}";
USER   };
USER }
USER ```
-------
ASSISTANT Ok, I will use that as the true, current contents of the files.
-------
USER I don't see the output of the service in the console. Is something missing?
-------
ASSISTANT Yes — the most likely culprit is the **kernel console log level**.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT `StandardOutput = "kmsg"` writes the service's output into the kernel log, but the kernel only *prints* log messages to the console whose priority is above `console_loglevel`. Userspace messages written via `/dev/kmsg` default to `LOG_INFO` (level 6), and the default `console_loglevel` is often 4–7 depending on the boot — so `info`-level output frequently gets **dropped from the console** even though it's in `dmesg`.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT You can confirm this inside the guest with:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT dmesg | grep -i agent   # or: journalctl -u llm-agent
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT If the output is there but not on the serial line, it's the log level.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **Fix** — raise the console log level via a kernel parameter in `llm-agent/setup.nix`:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT boot.kernelParams = [ "console=ttyS0" "loglevel=7" ];
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT (or `ignore_loglevel` if you want *everything* printed, which is noisy but useful for debugging).
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **Alternative fix** — skip `kmsg` entirely and let journald forward to the console, which bypasses kernel log-level filtering:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT services.journald.extraConfig = ''
ASSISTANT   ForwardToConsole=yes
ASSISTANT   MaxLevelConsole=info
ASSISTANT '';
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT with the service using plain `StandardOutput = "journal"`.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT I'd suggest trying `loglevel=7` first — it's a one-line change. If the output still doesn't appear, the next thing to check is that the service actually ran (`systemctl status llm-agent` inside the guest) and that it isn't failing before producing any output (e.g. the 9p mount or key file missing).
-------
USER I also want the vm to shutdown if the service terminates. How do I do that?
-------
ASSISTANT The cleanest way is a small companion unit that powers off the VM once the agent service reaches its terminal state. Since `llm-agent` is a `oneshot` with `RemainAfterExit`, a unit that `Requires` it and runs *after* it will only start once the agent has finished (successfully).
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Add this to the guest NixOS config in `llm-agent/setup.nix`:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT systemd.services.llm-agent-shutdown = {
ASSISTANT   description = "Power off the VM after the agent terminates";
ASSISTANT   wantedBy = [ "multi-user.target" ];
ASSISTANT   # Requires + After: only start once llm-agent has completed
ASSISTANT   requires = [ "llm-agent.service" ];
ASSISTANT   after = [ "llm-agent.service" ];
ASSISTANT   serviceConfig = {
ASSISTANT     Type = "oneshot";
ASSISTANT   };
ASSISTANT   script = ''
ASSISTANT     ${pkgs.systemd}/bin/systemctl poweroff
ASSISTANT   '';
ASSISTANT };
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT How it behaves:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT - **Agent succeeds** → `llm-agent.service` exits cleanly → `llm-agent-shutdown` starts → VM powers off.
ASSISTANT - **Agent fails** → because it `Requires` the agent service, the shutdown unit is pulled into the failure handling; with `Requires` alone a failed required unit stops dependents, so you may want `unitConfig.OnFailure` instead if you want poweroff on failure too:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT systemd.services.llm-agent.unitConfig.OnFailure = [ "poweroff.target" ];
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT (You can enable the built-in target with `systemd.targets.poweroff` — it exists by default; `OnFailure` referencing it works.)
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT A simpler alternative if you only care about the success path: drop `RemainAfterExit` and let the agent service itself end with:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT script = ''
ASSISTANT   ${agent-python}/bin/python ${agent-script}
ASSISTANT   ${pkgs.systemd}/bin/systemctl poweroff
ASSISTANT '';
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT but the companion-unit approach keeps the agent script free of VM lifecycle concerns, and handles failure via `OnFailure` more explicitly.
-------
USER The device 'devtools-config' does not show up in the vm. Therefore the shared mount is not available. Can you identify the cause?
-------
ASSISTANT A 9p/virtfs device is a bit unusual: it does **not** appear in `/dev` inside the guest — it's only accessible through a mount. So first, let's distinguish two failure modes:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **1. QEMU never got the `-virtfs` argument.**
ASSISTANT Check on the *host* while the VM is running:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT ps aux | grep -- '-virtfs'
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT If it's missing, the option isn't reaching the QEMU command line. `virtualisation.qemu.options` is baked into the generated `run-nixos-vm` script, so if it's missing there, the VM you're running is stale — rebuild (`nix run`/`nix build` again) after changing `setup.nix`.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **2. QEMU has the device, but the guest kernel can't see it.**
ASSISTANT The 9p virtio transport needs the `9pnet_virtio` (and `9p`) kernel modules. NixOS's `qemu-vm.nix` normally puts them in the initrd, but since you're mounting to `/run/agent-keys` (a regular systemd mount, not an initrd mount), the module must be loadable at runtime. Check inside the guest:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT lsmod | grep 9p
ASSISTANT modprobe 9pnet_virtio
ASSISTANT mount -t 9p -o trans=virtio,version=9p2000.L,ro devtools-config /run/agent-keys
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT If `modprobe` fails or the modules aren't present, add them explicitly:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT boot.initrd.availableKernelModules = [ "9p" "9pnet_virtio" ];
ASSISTANT boot.kernelModules = [ "9p" "9pnet_virtio" ];
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **One more thing worth checking:** you hardcoded the virtfs path to `/tmp`:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT ''-virtfs local,path="/tmp",mount_tag=devtools-config,...''
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT That's fine for QEMU, but note the launch script still writes the key into `$DEVTOOLS_CONFIG/users/` — so with `path=/tmp` the guest's `/run/agent-keys` will contain `/tmp`'s contents, *not* the key directory. If this was a debugging change, remember to restore `path="$DEVTOOLS_CONFIG"` (and keep the `export DEVTOOLS_CONFIG` in the launch script, since the run script inherits the environment) once the device shows up.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT My bet is on case 1 (stale VM build) or the missing kernel modules in case 2 — please run the `ps` check and let me know which it is.
-------
USER Manually mounting is working but it does not get mounted on boot. Do you know why?
-------
ASSISTANT The mount unit is generated by NixOS from your `fileSystems` entry, so the first step is to see what it actually says. Inside the guest:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT systemctl status run-run-agent-keys.mount
ASSISTANT journalctl -u run-run-agent-keys.mount -b
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT The unit name is the mount path with slashes turned into dashes: `/run/agent-keys` → `run-run-agent-keys.mount`.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT The typical failure in this exact setup (QEMU VM, 9p, stage-2 systemd mount) is one of:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **1. The 9p modules aren't loaded when systemd attempts the mount.**
ASSISTANT Systemd mounts `local-fs.target` units very early in stage 2. The kernel *can* autoload `9p`/`9pnet_virtio` via `request_module`, but only if the modules are in the guest's module closure and kmod is functional — and in the minimal VM environment this often silently fails, giving `unknown filesystem type '9p'' in the journal. Since your manual `modprobe` + `mount` works, the modules are present — they're just not loaded at mount time. Fix:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT boot.initrd.availableKernelModules = [ "9p" "9pnet_virtio" ];
ASSISTANT boot.kernelModules = [ "9p" "9pnet_virtio" ];
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT `boot.kernelModules` makes them load via `systemd-modules-load` early in boot; `initrd.availableKernelModules` covers the case where the mount gets pulled into the initrd.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **2. The mount unit is being started before the virtio device is usable.**
ASSISTANT Less likely, but if the journal shows a timeout rather than an fs-type error, add an explicit dependency so the mount waits for the virtio transport:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT fileSystems."/run/agent-keys" = {
ASSISTANT   device = "devtools-config";
ASSISTANT   fsType = "9p";
ASSISTANT   options = [ "trans=virtio" "version=9p2000.L" "ro" ];
ASSISTANT };
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT is otherwise fine — 9p with `trans=virtio` doesn't need a `/dev` node, so no udev waiting is involved.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **3. Stale VM image.**
ASSISTANT If the journal shows *no* mount unit at all, the VM you booted was built before you added the `fileSystems` entry — rebuild and re-run `nix run .#devtools/<agent>`.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Please run the `systemctl status` / `journalctl` commands and paste the error — that will tell us definitively which of these it is. My money is on (1).
-------
USER I think the nixos vm is ignoring the filesystem entry. It dosen't panic with invalid values.
-------
ASSISTANT That's the clue — and it's in your current file. Look at the `fileSystems` entry:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT fileSystems."/run/agent-keys" = {
ASSISTANT   enable = true;
ASSISTANT   device = "devtools-config";
ASSISTANT   fsType = "ext4";
ASSISTANT   options = [ "nofail" ];
ASSISTANT   #fsType = "9p";
ASSISTANT   #options = [ "trans=virtio" "version=9p2000.L" "ro" "nofail" ];
ASSISTANT };
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT The 9p lines are commented out and the entry currently says `fsType = "ext4"`. With that configuration:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT - systemd generates a mount unit for a **block device** named `devtools-config`,
ASSISTANT - no such block device ever exists (it's a virtfs tag, not a disk),
ASSISTANT - because of `nofail`, the boot **doesn't fail or panic** — the mount unit just times out waiting for the device and is silently skipped.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT So the entry isn't being ignored by NixOS — it's being generated as an ext4 mount that can never succeed, and `nofail` suppresses the error.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Restore the 9p configuration:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT fileSystems."/run/agent-keys" = {
ASSISTANT   device = "devtools-config";
ASSISTANT   fsType = "9p";
ASSISTANT   options = [ "trans=virtio" "version=9p2000.L" "ro" ];
ASSISTANT };
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT (keep `boot.kernelModules`/`boot.initrd.availableKernelModules` with `9p`/`9pnet_virtio` so the modules are loaded before the mount runs), rebuild, and the mount should appear at boot — you can verify with `systemctl status run-run-agent-keys.mount` in the guest.
-------
USER I removed nofail same result
-------
ASSISTANT If the mount unit doesn't exist *at all* in the guest (rather than failing), then the VM is running a **stale system closure** — the `fileSystems` change never made it into the image you're booting.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Please check inside the guest:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT systemctl list-units --all | grep -i agent-keys
ASSISTANT systemctl cat run-run-agent-keys.mount
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT - If `systemctl cat` says the unit doesn't exist → the guest closure predates your change. The `run-nixos-vm` script boots `vm.config.system.build.vm`, which embeds the *evaluated* NixOS configuration — so any change to `fileSystems` requires re-evaluating and re-running the flake app (`nix run .#devtools/<agent>` or `nix build` first). If you re-ran an old `result` symlink or a previously-built `run-nixos-vm` script, you get the old config.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT - If the unit *does* exist but fails/hangs → then it's a real mount problem, and I'd want the output of:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT journalctl -u run-run-agent-keys.mount -b
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT One more thing to double-check on the host: make sure the `fsType = "9p"` / `options = [ "trans=virtio" ... ]` lines are actually uncommented in the file you're building from (your pasted version had them commented out with `ext4` active). With `ext4` and no `nofail`, boot would *hang* waiting for a nonexistent block device — if your boot instead proceeds normally to a login prompt, that strongly suggests the guest is running a closure that doesn't contain your `fileSystems` entry at all, i.e. the stale-build case.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Can you run those two `systemctl` commands and tell me what you get?
-------
USER I've re-evaluated the nixos expression, same result.
-------
ASSISTANT Let's verify where the entry gets lost — on the *host*, before even booting the VM. Two decisive checks:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **1. Is the option actually set in the evaluated configuration?**
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT nix eval .#<your-vm-output>.config.fileSystems."/run/agent-keys"
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT (or, if you have a shell into the flake, evaluate `vm.config.fileSystems."/run/agent-keys"` however you expose it). If this prints your 9p entry, evaluation is fine and the problem is on the boot side. If it errors or prints something else, the entry is being overridden/lost during evaluation.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **2. Is the mount unit in the built closure?**
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT nix build .#<vm-system>  # or whatever builds vm.config.system.build.vm
ASSISTANT find ./result -name '*agent-keys*'
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT You should find `run-run-agent-keys.mount` (and possibly a `.device` symlink) in the systemd unit directory of the closure. If the unit file is there but doesn't show up in the booted guest, the guest is running a different closure than the one you just built — e.g. the `run-nixos-vm` script you're executing is an old one on your `PATH` or in a `result` symlink, rather than the freshly built one.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **3. In the guest**, confirm what systemd actually knows:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT systemctl list-units --all --type=mount
ASSISTANT systemd-fstab-generator --dry-run 2>&1   # not needed, skip
ASSISTANT cat /etc/fstab | grep agent-keys
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT The `/etc/fstab` check is the most telling: NixOS generates `/etc/fstab` from `fileSystems`. If `/run/agent-keys` is **absent from the guest's `/etc/fstab`**, the booted system simply is not the one you evaluated — no amount of re-mounting logic can fix that, and it points squarely at a stale build/script being executed.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT My suspicion, given "re-evaluated, same result": the `launch-llm-agent` script embeds `${vm.config.system.build.vm}/bin/run-nixos-vm` — if the flake app you run (`nix run .#devtools/<agent>`) was cached by nix (flake inputs/`nix run` doesn't rebuild if nothing in the *flake* changed... it should, since setup.nix is in the flake). But if you're invoking an old `result` symlink or a copied script, you'd get exactly this behavior.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Please run check 3 (`cat /etc/fstab`) in the guest and check 2 on the host, and tell me what you find.
-------
USER The mount unit dosen't exist nor it in fstab. For some reason, qemu ignores the filesystems. Are they being overriden by the qemu-vm module? As you can see, even setting fake options has no effect.
-------
ASSISTANT No — `qemu-vm.nix` does **not** override or wipe `fileSystems`. It only *adds* entries (like `/` via the `virtualisation.fileSystems` option). Your entries would be merged in, not discarded.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT And your experiment proves something stronger: you set
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT fileSystems."/run/agent-keysxx" = 5;
ASSISTANT fileSystems."/run/agent-keys".fakeOption = "no";
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT If that module were being **evaluated at all**, NixOS would throw immediately — `fileSystems` is `attrsOf (submodule ...)`, so an integer value and an unknown option are hard type errors. The fact that evaluation succeeds silently means **the module containing these settings is not part of the system being evaluated/built**. The VM you're running was never generated from this code.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT So the question becomes: why isn't this module in the evaluation? Things to check, in order of likelihood:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **1. Is `llm-agent/setup.nix` actually imported/instantiated by the flake module?**
ASSISTANT Check `llm-agent/config.nix` and `flakeModule.nix`/`presets/flakeModule.nix` — is the setup module wired in only when an agent is *declared* in the flake config? If no `llm-agent` entry exists in the config (or the name doesn't match), the module may never be instantiated, and the app you're running comes from somewhere else entirely.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **2. Are you running the app this file produces?**
ASSISTANT Verify what `nix run` actually executes:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT nix eval .#apps.<system>.devtools/<agent-name>.program
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT and compare its store path freshness with a fresh build. If you're invoking an old `result` symlink or a script on your PATH, you'll boot a stale closure no matter how often you re-evaluate.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **3. Force-evaluate the VM config directly** to see whether your entries exist:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT nix eval <the-vm-attr>.config.fileSystems."/run/agent-keys" --json
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT (or add a temporary `builtins.trace`/`assert` in `setup.nix`, e.g. `assert builtins.trace (attrNames config.fileSystems) true;` style debugging in the module). If evaluation of that attribute fails with the type errors above — good, the module *is* live and you just have a build/run mismatch. If it evaluates cleanly without your entries, the module is dead code.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT My bet is on (1): the `llm-agent` flake module isn't instantiating `setup.nix` for your current configuration, so the `run-nixos-vm` you're launching was built from a different (older or default) definition. Could you add `llm-agent/config.nix` and the relevant part of `flakeModule.nix` to the chat so I can check the wiring?
-------
USER Can it be converted to a systemd mount and make the llm-agent service depend on it?
USER 
USER Reply in English.
LLM RESPONSE 2026-10-27T16:22:42
ASSISTANT <thinking-content-7bbeb8e1441453ad999a0bbba8a46d4b>
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT User wants to mount via a systemd mount unit defined manually and make llm-agent depend on it.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT </thinking-content-7bbeb8e1441453ad999a0bbba8a46d4b>
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Yes — you can define the mount as an explicit systemd unit and wire the agent service to it. This bypasses the `fileSystems`/fstab generator entirely, which is useful given your current debugging situation.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT In the guest NixOS config in `llm-agent/setup.nix`:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT systemd.mounts = [
ASSISTANT   {
ASSISTANT     where = "/run/agent-keys";
ASSISTANT     what = "devtools-config";
ASSISTANT     type = "9p";
ASSISTANT     options = "trans=virtio,version=9p2000.L,ro";
ASSISTANT     wantedBy = [ "multi-user.target" ];
ASSISTANT     mountConfig = {
ASSISTANT       DirectoryMode = "0755";
ASSISTANT     };
ASSISTANT   }
ASSISTANT ];
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Notes:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT - `systemd.mounts` entries generate a unit named after the path (`run-run-agent-keys.mount`) and, with `wantedBy = [ "multi-user.target" ]`, it's pulled in at boot.
ASSISTANT - The mount directory `/run/agent-keys` is created automatically by systemd (`RuntimeDirectory` semantics apply to `Where=` paths under `/run` — systemd creates missing parent dirs for mount points).
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Then make the agent service depend on it:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ```nix
ASSISTANT systemd.services.llm-agent = {
ASSISTANT   description = "LangChain deep agent";
ASSISTANT   wantedBy = [ "multi-user.target" ];
ASSISTANT   requires = [ "run-run-agent-keys.mount" ];
ASSISTANT   after = [ "network-online.target" "run-run-agent-keys.mount" ];
ASSISTANT   wants = [ "network-online.target" ];
ASSISTANT   environment = { ... };   # unchanged
ASSISTANT   serviceConfig = { ... }; # unchanged
ASSISTANT   script = ''...'';       # unchanged
ASSISTANT };
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT `Requires` + `After` means: the agent won't start until the 9p mount is up, and if the mount fails, the agent service fails too (which pairs well with your `OnFailure = poweroff.target` idea — the VM would shut down if the key share can't be mounted).
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT One caveat: since you're dropping the `fileSystems` entry, remove it (or keep them consistent) to avoid two competing definitions of the same mount point. Also keep `boot.kernelModules = [ "9p" "9pnet_virtio" ];` so the modules are loaded before systemd attempts the mount.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT That said — this will only work if the module is actually being evaluated. Your `fakeOption`/`fileSystems."/..." = 5` experiment strongly suggests the whole `setup.nix` module isn't in the closure you're booting; if so, a `systemd.mounts` entry will be silently ignored exactly the same way. I'd still recommend verifying the wiring (`llm-agent/config.nix` / `flakeModule.nix`) before or after making this change.
